@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# HaloDeck — Native Halo CE for Steam Deck & Linux
+
+This repository contains the reverse-engineered Halo: Combat Evolved native Linux port, optimized and tested for Valve Steam Deck (SteamOS) and x86_64 Linux. It builds on the Linux port described in [README.md](README.md) and [port/linux/README.md](port/linux/README.md) (controls, settings, system link).
+
+No game data is included: you need the maps from your own copy of the Xbox game.
+
+## Features & Fixes
+- **Retail Map Compatibility**: Supports retail USA Rev-2 maps (`01.10.12.2276`) alongside build `01.01.14.2342`.
+- **Steam Deck Launcher**: Ships `port/linux/launch_halo.sh`, which starts the game from its own folder, where it finds its maps and libraries. The game starts fullscreen, as Gamescope expects, and uses Mesa's GL thread by default.
+- **Self-contained game folder**: the executable looks for its libraries in its own folder (and in `lib/` there) before the system's (an `$ORIGIN` run path), so a 32-bit `libSDL3.so.0` copied next to it is found without installing anything.
+
+## Building
+
+Prerequisites:
+- Python 3 and the Ninja build system
+- Clang and LLD (the default build is link-time optimised, and links with `ld.lld`)
+- 32-bit glibc development files (`lib32-glibc` on Arch, `gcc-multilib` on Debian/Ubuntu)
+- 32-bit SDL3 (`lib32-sdl3` on Arch, `libsdl3-dev:i386` on Debian/Ubuntu)
+
+```sh
+python configure.py --portable
+ninja linux
+```
+The output executable will be placed in `build/linux/halo`.
+
+`--portable` builds for any x86-64 processor. Without it the build is optimised for the processor of the computer that builds it (`-march=native`), and may not start on a Steam Deck if it was built on another computer. Add `--release` for a build that, like the retail game, does not stop at the first failed assertion.
+
+If the 32-bit SDL3 is not installed where the linker looks (for example because it was unpacked from the `lib32-sdl3` package into `lib32/` on a Steam Deck, whose system files are read-only), tell `configure.py` where it is:
+
+```sh
+python configure.py --portable --linux-lib-dir lib32/usr/lib32
+```
+
+That library is only used for linking: the game finds it at run time once it is copied next to `halo`, as below (or into `build/linux/` to run the game in place).
+
+## Steam Deck Deployment
+
+1. Create a game folder on your Steam Deck (e.g. `~/Games/HaloCE/`).
+2. Copy `build/linux/halo`, `port/linux/launch_halo.sh`, and the 32-bit `libSDL3.so.0` to that folder. The library is in `/usr/lib32/` on Arch, `/usr/lib/i386-linux-gnu/` on Debian/Ubuntu, or `lib32/usr/lib32/` if you unpacked it there. Copy the library itself, not a symbolic link to it: `cp -L /usr/lib32/libSDL3.so.0 ~/Games/HaloCE/`.
+3. Place your extracted Xbox Halo CE maps in `maps/` (or `assets/maps/`) in that folder.
+4. Add `launch_halo.sh` as a Non-Steam Game in Steam Desktop Mode.
+5. In Game Mode, launch directly with native controller support.
+
+The game writes its settings to `config.toml` next to `halo` on its first run (see [Settings](port/linux/README.md#settings)); a Steam launch option such as `HALO_FULLSCREEN=false %command%` overrides one for a single run. Saved games and the game's cache of copied maps (about 800 MB) go to `~/.local/share/halo-linux`.
+
+## Credits
+
+HaloDeck is built on other people's work:
+
+- **punpckhdq** started the Halo: Combat Evolved decompilation ([punpckhdq/halo](https://github.com/punpckhdq/halo)), and **bnunu** continued it ([bnunu/halo](https://github.com/bnunu/halo)).
+- **MrBruh** ([@cybersecurity](https://github.com/cybersecurity)) made the native Linux, Windows and Android ports this builds on ([halo-ce-universal](https://github.com/cybersecurity/halo-ce-universal)).
+- **Jonas Volman** did much of the decompilation's matching work and the 128-player system link support.
+- Everyone else who has contributed to those projects.
+- The port uses [SDL3](https://libsdl.org) (zlib license), [tomlc17](https://github.com/cktan/tomlc17) by CK Tan (MIT license) and libtiff by Sam Leffler and Silicon Graphics (libtiff license).
+- Steam Deck launcher, notes and fixes: **Jmesmykil**.
+
+Halo: Combat Evolved was developed by Bungie and published by Microsoft. HaloDeck is an unofficial fan project, not affiliated with or endorsed by Microsoft, Xbox Game Studios, Halo Studios or Bungie. Halo, Xbox and Microsoft are trademarks of the Microsoft group of companies.
